    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ PART H — Calendars -->
    <h2 class="sec" id="part-h-calendars-of-the-world-how-humans-have-counted-time">Part H · calendars of the world — how humans have counted time</h2>

    <!-- Introduction panel -->
    <div class="content-panel u-mb-md">
      <div class="panel-title">Why do so many calendars exist?</div>
      <p class="muted-copy-large">Every calendar is a civilisation's answer to the same three astronomical cycles: the solar year (~365.25 days), the lunar month (~29.53 days), and the day. Because these cycles do not divide evenly into each other, every calendar system involves a compromise -- and different cultures made different choices. Today more than 40 calendar systems are in active use worldwide.</p>
      <div class="u-display-grid u-gridcols-auto-fit-130px-1fr u-gap-md u-mt-md">
        <div class="sub-card">
          <div class="mini-card-title">Solar calendars</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight">Track the Sun. Seasons are stable. Gregorian, Persian Shamsi.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-card">
          <div class="mini-card-title">Lunar calendars</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight">Track the Moon. Months drift through all seasons over ~33 years. Islamic Hijri.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-card">
          <div class="mini-card-title">Lunisolar calendars</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight">Track both, inserting leap months to re-sync. Hebrew, Chinese.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Five Calendar Tabs -->
    <div class="content-panel u-mb-md">
      <div class="panel-title u-mb-md">Five major calendars in depth</div>
      <div class="button-row" id="cal-tab-btns"></div>
      <div id="cal-tab-content"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Calendar year length comparison SVG -->
    <div class="content-panel u-mb-md">
      <div class="panel-title">Year length comparison -- solar vs lunar drift</div>
      <div class="muted-copy u-mb-md">Over 33 years, a pure lunar calendar loses a full year relative to the solar calendar. This is why Islamic holy months cycle through all seasons.</div>
      <div class="svg-wrap">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 660 160" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="u-w-100-pct u-maxw-700px u-display-block">
