    <!-- Interactive: Today's date in 5 calendars -->
    <div class="content-panel u-mb-md">
      <div class="panel-title">Today's date in every major calendar</div>
      <div class="muted-copy u-mb-md">The same moment on Earth, expressed in five different counting systems. Pick any Gregorian date to see it converted.</div>
      <div class="u-display-flex u-gap-md u-items-center u-flex-wrap u-mb-md">
        <label for="cal-date-input" class="u-fs-13px text-secondary">Gregorian date:</label>
        <input type="date" id="cal-date-input" class="u-fs-13px" oninput="renderCalDates()">
        <button onclick="document.getElementById('cal-date-input').valueAsDate = new Date(); renderCalDates();" class="u-fs-12px">Today</button>
      </div>
      <div id="cal-dates-grid" class="u-display-grid u-gridcols-auto-fit-130px-1fr u-gap-md"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- Calendar facts anchor list -->
    <div class="content-panel u-mb-lg">
      <div class="panel-title u-mb-sm">Things worth knowing about calendars</div>
      <div class="anchor-row">
        <span class="pill p-blue u-minw-70px">Gregorian</span>
        <div><div class="atext">The Gregorian calendar was introduced by Pope Gregory XIII in 1582 to fix a 10-day drift in the Julian calendar. Catholic countries switched immediately; Protestant Britain did not adopt it until 1752 -- by which point 11 days had to be dropped. People rioted in the streets shouting "Give us back our 11 days."</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="anchor-row">
        <span class="pill p-amber u-minw-70px">Islamic</span>
        <div><div class="atext">Because the Hijri year is 11 days shorter than the solar year, Ramadan shifts through all seasons in a cycle of roughly 33 years. A Muslim born in 1990 will have experienced Ramadan in summer, autumn, winter, and spring before age 33. This is considered theologically significant: everyone must experience fasting under both easy and hard conditions.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="anchor-row">
        <span class="pill p-teal u-minw-70px">Persian</span>
        <div><div class="atext">The Persian Shamsi calendar is widely considered the most astronomically accurate calendar in regular use. Its leap year rule -- adding a leap day in specific patterns over a 2,820-year grand cycle -- keeps it within one day of the true solar year for that entire period, compared to the Gregorian calendar's periodic drift. Nowruz (New Year) always falls on or within one day of the March equinox.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="anchor-row">
        <span class="pill p-purple u-minw-70px">Hebrew</span>
        <div><div class="atext">The Hebrew calendar inserts a 13th "leap month" (Adar II) in 7 out of every 19 years -- the Metonic cycle, which nearly perfectly synchronises the solar and lunar cycles. This ensures Jewish holidays always fall in the same season: Passover is always in spring, Sukkot in autumn.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="anchor-row">
        <span class="pill p-amber u-minw-70px">Chinese</span>
        <div><div class="atext">The Chinese lunisolar calendar underlies one of the world's most famous cultural cycles: the 12-year animal zodiac (rat, ox, tiger...) combined with a 10-stem cycle producing a 60-year "sexagenary cycle." The calendar also defines 24 solar terms (jieqi), which historically guided agricultural decisions. Chinese New Year falls between January 21 and February 20 each year -- the second new moon after the winter solstice.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    

